@page "/mcp-server"

@* Describes the MCP server this demo's host maps at /mcp. Its route differs from the endpoint's on
   purpose: two literal endpoints on /mcp would make a GET of it ambiguous. *@

<PageTitle>MCP server</PageTitle>

<div class="demo-intro">
    <h1>MCP server</h1>
    <p>
        This site hosts a Model Context Protocol server at <code>/mcp</code>, so an AI agent writing form code with
        Bit.Borm reads the library itself - its compiled API documentation, its guide, its stylesheet, its own JSON
        Schema importer and these demo pages - instead of guessing at an API it half remembers. Every tool is read-only.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="demo-card mcp-card">
    <h2>Connect a client</h2>

    <p>
        The endpoint is plain Streamable HTTP, so any MCP client connects with a URL and nothing else. The same tools
        are also served, version by version, through the bit platform's own server at
        <code>https://bitplatform.dev/mcp</code>, next to the other bit libraries.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-source">
        <pre><code>@ClientConfig</code></pre>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="demo-card mcp-card">
    <h2>Tools</h2>

    <table class="mcp-table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it answers</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>SearchBorm</code></td>
                <td>Everything at once - the guide, every public member, the setup guides, the theme, the stylesheet and the demo's working forms - each hit with the exact follow-up call that returns its full text.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBormSetupGuide</code></td>
                <td>The complete wiring for one hosting model: web-app, webassembly, server or hybrid - <code>AddBorm()</code> in every container that renders a form, and the stylesheet link.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBormGuideSection</code></td>
                <td>One section of the reference guide, or the index of sections.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBormApi</code></td>
                <td>One public type with every parameter, property and method, its real default and its documentation - or the index of types.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBormTheme</code></td>
                <td>The <code>--bit-borm-*</code> custom properties with their light and dark values and what each one is for - or one of them in full, with the override to write in every colour mode.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>InspectBormSchema</code></td>
                <td>What the library makes of a JSON Schema document - the form it becomes, field by field, what <code>Validate()</code> reports, what the importer drops without a word, and the document it exports back - or a sample schema, to see the format.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>GetBormSourceFile</code></td>
                <td>One demo or library file verbatim - every page here is a working form - or the index of files.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        The guide, the API, the theme, a sample schema and the sources are also exposed as <code>borm://</code>
        resources, and three prompts - <code>add-borm-to-app</code>, <code>implement-borm-form</code> and
        <code>debug-borm-form</code> - walk an agent through the common jobs in the right order.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="demo-card mcp-card">
    <h2>The same tools over plain HTTP</h2>

    <p>
        Every tool is also a <code>GET</code> under <code>/api/mcp/...</code>: open one in a browser tab to read
        exactly what an agent is handed.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/SearchBorm?query=cascading%20options" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/SearchBorm?query=cascading options</a></li>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/GetBormApi?typeName=BormState" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/GetBormApi?typeName=BormState</a></li>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/GetBormTheme" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/GetBormTheme</a></li>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/InspectBormSchema" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/InspectBormSchema</a></li>
        <li><a href="/api/mcp/GetBormSourceFile?path=Demo/Client/Pages/WizardDemo.razor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">/api/mcp/GetBormSourceFile?path=Demo/Client/Pages/WizardDemo.razor</a></li>
    </ul>
</section>

@* The demo's stylesheet has no table styles of its own - every other page is a form - so the few this
   page needs live with it, built on the site's own --demo-* palette. *@
<style>
    .mcp-card {
        margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
        line-height: 1.55;
    }

    .mcp-card h2 {
        margin: 0 0 .6rem;
        font-size: 1.15rem;
    }

    .mcp-card p {
        margin: .4rem 0;
        color: var(--demo-muted);
    }

    .mcp-card .demo-source {
        margin-top: .75rem;
    }

    .mcp-table {
        width: 100%;
        border-collapse: collapse;
        font-size: .9rem;
        margin-bottom: .6rem;
    }

    .mcp-table th,
    .mcp-table td {
        padding: .45rem .6rem;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--demo-border);
        text-align: left;
        vertical-align: top;
    }

    .mcp-table td:first-child {
        white-space: nowrap;
    }
</style>

@code {
    private const string ClientConfig = """
        // .mcp.json (Claude Code, VS Code, and anything else reading this shape)
        {
          "mcpServers": {
            "borm": {
              "type": "http",
              "url": "https://borm.bitplatform.dev/mcp"
            }
          }
        }
        """;
}
